#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166488 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166883 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d13b0d31-65b8-550f-b97b-f4ece63bf834" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "22a44ff4-55e7-51db-a54e-26ef23764fa4" :question/math-academy-id "e-12490" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y) =\\dfrac{xy}2, \\qquad\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2y, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 4 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $a$ given that $b=2.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A = f(a,b) \\cdot \\textrm{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}\\frac{xy}{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{4}]_{x=0}^{x=2y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}]_{y=0}^{y=4} \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[x]_{x=0}^{x=2y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=4} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=2.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}64 & =f(a,2)\\cdot 16 \\\\ 64 & =\\frac{a\\cdot 2}{2}\\cdot 16 \\\\ 64 & =16a \\\\ a & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\left( 4,2 \\right) \\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a = 4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166489 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166884 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b43eea5b-9da3-5fe0-a28c-cc01aed3627a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Average Value of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "0a06a8ae-69d6-5ded-979e-149f650a1676" :question/math-academy-id "e-12491" :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y) = xy$ on the region $D = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -1 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: x \\leq y \\leq 3x \\big\\}$ given that the area of the region is $3$ square units." :question/worked-solution "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{x}^{3x}xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}[\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{2}]_{y=x}^{y=3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[{x}^{4}]_{x=-1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(16-1) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166490 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166885 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4e5deee-e910-5bb1-9d9a-0b24886ab6c1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Average Value of a Function Over a Polar Region"}] :question/id #uuid "13800660-08b4-50af-93b4-1013bbf41e66" :question/math-academy-id "e-12492" :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y) = 15xy^2$ on the portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at the origin, where $x\\geq 0,$ and $y \\leq 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region $D\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n![](images/a3/a381fede820f9fc5ba08bb9569906d41a3e8f779de38b446c4f271ceef3a5ccd.png)\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D) = \\dfrac{1}{4} \\cdot \\pi \\cdot 1^2 = \\dfrac\\pi 4.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_{\\textrm{ave}} = \\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{Area}(D)} \\, \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2} \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}15x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}15r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}15{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}5{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{[{r}^{5}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ({1}^{5}-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta ]}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =({sin}^{3}⁡(2\\pi )-{sin}^{3}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =(0-(-1)) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nf_{\\textrm{ave}} = \\dfrac{4}{\\pi} \\cdot 1 = \\dfrac{4}{\\pi}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166884 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b43eea5b-9da3-5fe0-a28c-cc01aed3627a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Average Value of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08263df7-a90a-5d1e-9ef0-1b115c0bcf18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{80}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278664} {:answer/id #uuid "173ca95b-d1d5-55a8-a28d-2f15919a4060" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278800} {:answer/id #uuid "4c4b694d-2a3e-51c2-9eb8-1f26a851e476" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{42}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279274} {:answer/id #uuid "b6cf2e9c-9ccf-5ec1-af35-0d3039c9ab61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280196} {:answer/id #uuid "d5e09d1e-8efd-55dc-b004-c5905609fe0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08263df7-a90a-5d1e-9ef0-1b115c0bcf18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{80}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278664} :answer-field/id #uuid "b007d617-0d88-5bd3-8546-df32ba381651" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b5fbae5f-d41d-5595-bcf0-ce1fbfd9706b" :question/math-academy-id "q-163063" :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-4{y}^{3}$ on the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=1\\cdot 3=3.\n$$\n\nApplying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-4{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{2}-4{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{x}^{2}y-{y}^{4}]_{y=0}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{x}^{2}-81)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[{x}^{3}-81x]_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({1}^{3}-81\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{80}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167135 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166884 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b43eea5b-9da3-5fe0-a28c-cc01aed3627a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Average Value of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94baf68a-d3c4-5d97-bf9b-0ccaba1b5859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163889} {:answer/id #uuid "ab56f8ea-1480-51be-8914-aa9a0232c26b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163890} {:answer/id #uuid "f3bb76c1-b6ce-5232-906e-46ec397e0d6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163891} {:answer/id #uuid "fd36f9ef-db3d-56b4-9d88-5fed8f3ac55e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163892} {:answer/id #uuid "6734fb2e-a070-553e-813c-5f7158f56e02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3bb76c1-b6ce-5232-906e-46ec397e0d6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163891} :answer-field/id #uuid "f854d318-2b28-51b8-8b9b-478cde98f4a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e9cfd5e0-f192-510e-add3-93b96f5880ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-163064" :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)=14x{y}^{2}$ on the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}$ given that the area of the region is $\\frac{1}{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}{∬}_{D}14x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}14x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}[7{x}^{2}{y}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x={y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}7{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3[{y}^{7}]_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =3(1-0) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167137 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166885 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4e5deee-e910-5bb1-9d9a-0b24886ab6c1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Average Value of a Function Over a Polar Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c1ea06c-900b-518b-90c1-188a82af18c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163902} {:answer/id #uuid "4486d7e0-2a87-59a4-9e31-7eb1fae5055e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163904} {:answer/id #uuid "3243821a-d8ad-5dd7-aced-e15740550519" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480836} {:answer/id #uuid "932abe94-bffd-5dad-a100-ed7dd0878970" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481015} {:answer/id #uuid "a461f542-25f5-59e6-a3ba-329bd57b7ee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "932abe94-bffd-5dad-a100-ed7dd0878970" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481015} :answer-field/id #uuid "a885e1f2-0379-5509-836d-b0ab6acba537" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e7ccaa25-bf49-56f7-93ee-b9d797d03126" :question/math-academy-id "q-163068" :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)=2x+y$ on the portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin, where $y\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region $D:$ \n\n![](images/b5/b5ba69ab02aa13e201b7b9113f6a36e705ad19e6412499504afc4f239459a8f3.png)\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}=2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,{[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}((2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ))-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}(1+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{2\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{16}{3}=\\frac{8}{3\\pi }.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481403 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166883 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d13b0d31-65b8-550f-b97b-f4ece63bf834" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0af1d0d8-4d56-5266-bc41-27fa5b76576a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480762} {:answer/id #uuid "785b057b-4af7-5e4d-9df3-d94e6d6ae85a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480959} {:answer/id #uuid "85fad9c8-dc51-56ac-8cfd-ba8871833f87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480998} {:answer/id #uuid "872e6360-ee60-5504-b2e0-a14058beef80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481001} {:answer/id #uuid "c776c1f8-e8aa-56f3-b7bd-cc975451f08b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481119}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c776c1f8-e8aa-56f3-b7bd-cc975451f08b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481119} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e005fc3-8464-5bd2-9ea3-7db99a984a04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a8b13a1a-6bdb-56d5-b06e-dfa9901c1db1" :question/math-academy-id "q-163149" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x+3{y}^{2},\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $a$ given that $b=1.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(2x+3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x+3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[2xy+{y}^{3}]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4x+8)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[2{x}^{2}+8x]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot {2}^{2}+8\\cdot 2)-0 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=2\\cdot 2=4.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=1.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24 & =f(a,1)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 24 & =(2a+3\\cdot {1}^{2})\\cdot 4 \\\\ 6 & =2a+3 \\\\ 3 & =2a \\\\ a & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(\\frac{3}{2},1)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a=\\frac{3}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481399 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166883 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d13b0d31-65b8-550f-b97b-f4ece63bf834" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50b7b909-d962-51d6-9392-bbe5c3a4f0d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480886} {:answer/id #uuid "6bf4c5c6-de83-56d9-bcdb-6036594d8b2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480942} {:answer/id #uuid "95973894-ec85-559b-89b7-bafc61605c0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481022} {:answer/id #uuid "e1ffe2e3-2de3-51af-a5e8-e086627cabd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481165} {:answer/id #uuid "ea4ec8f7-1073-567c-82c5-5f1ecde10a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea4ec8f7-1073-567c-82c5-5f1ecde10a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481189} :answer-field/id #uuid "db0e353a-fec5-5d61-839e-8dfe38eddf51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481324}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a130ee36-c4fa-557a-b62c-af4b4bf44ed4" :question/math-academy-id "q-163151" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x+{y}^{2},\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $a$ given that $b=1.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(2x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(2x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[2xy+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]_{y=0}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(6x+9)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[3{x}^{2}+9x]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot {2}^{2}+9\\cdot 2)-0 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=2\\cdot 3=6.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=1.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30 & =f(a,1)\\cdot 6 \\\\ 30 & =(2a+{1}^{2})\\cdot 6 \\\\ 30 & =12a+6 \\\\ 24 & =12a \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(2,1)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481367 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166883 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d13b0d31-65b8-550f-b97b-f4ece63bf834" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0794f66a-f45d-5607-98bd-140c6175658d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480756} {:answer/id #uuid "2b32d836-4282-5cc7-9a51-0ec7993b4598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480819} {:answer/id #uuid "5932c544-2818-549e-b0bc-0ffb7089e13f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480901} {:answer/id #uuid "bc7fc21c-1500-5e17-a1b2-d0a8f29d6724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481099} {:answer/id #uuid "f814775e-bc0c-5f32-a98d-179b8afcfcc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b32d836-4282-5cc7-9a51-0ec7993b4598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480819} :answer-field/id #uuid "421edb72-f508-5f8a-ab68-a626bb9c3ff3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "510822f4-2240-59da-8db4-5275b43b2562" :question/math-academy-id "q-163197" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2xy,\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{y}{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $a$ given that $b=3.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{y/2}2xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[{x}^{2}y]_{x=0}^{x=y/2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{{y}^{3}}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[\\frac{{y}^{4}}{16}]_{y=0}^{y=4} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{y/2}\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[x]_{x=0}^{x=y/2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{y}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=4} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=3.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16 & =f(a,3)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 16 & =(2a\\cdot 3)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 16 & =24a \\\\ a & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(\\frac{2}{3},3)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a=\\frac{2}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481421 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166883 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d13b0d31-65b8-550f-b97b-f4ece63bf834" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2edca394-1295-57f0-b5af-ed3335a801ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480827} {:answer/id #uuid "5a9d8a94-9534-5e68-924f-dcf67bae6e63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480906} {:answer/id #uuid "9596a5ea-3eda-5d12-a885-036340592f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481021} {:answer/id #uuid "9993e960-d9b3-5a9c-a231-8dca7a1500f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481034} {:answer/id #uuid "c0180c32-42f3-525a-b857-b3753cd90618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481105}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9596a5ea-3eda-5d12-a885-036340592f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481021} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c26abb0-e3c4-5856-80ba-2321788c3c2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481276}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d1845eab-f52b-5f23-bd27-faf40f79c71a" :question/math-academy-id "q-163205" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x+y,\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le y,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 6\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $a$ given that $b=4.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}{\\int }_{y/2}^{y}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}[{x}^{2}+xy]_{x=y/2}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}([{y}^{2}+{y}^{2}]-[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{5{y}^{3}}{12}]}_{y=0}^{y=6} \\\\  & =90.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}{\\int }_{y/2}^{y}\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}[x]_{x=y/2}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{y}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=6} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=4.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}90 & =f(a,4)\\cdot 9 \\\\ 90 & =(2a+4)\\cdot 9 \\\\ 10 & =2a+4 \\\\ 6 & =2a \\\\ a & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(3,4)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481378 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166883 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d13b0d31-65b8-550f-b97b-f4ece63bf834" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "071427db-1ab5-5a7b-8ab5-fd9f44da6576" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480755} {:answer/id #uuid "38e562f4-0b20-5eaa-a03e-6faae7854a82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480850} {:answer/id #uuid "a7b6ca86-eebb-5760-b9bd-8e54ad1102c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481056} {:answer/id #uuid "b5822c67-2544-5801-8e37-04b814eeeae7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481084} {:answer/id #uuid "e1a845bb-0954-584e-b542-37d3783851e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481163}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1a845bb-0954-584e-b542-37d3783851e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481163} :answer-field/id #uuid "efe2ad41-143d-5a5f-9acb-57cf8643ab94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "65bd6053-5f41-5a6f-ac31-815596c779fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-163208" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=3y-2x,\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le y,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $b$ given that $a=1.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(3y-2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{y}(3y-2x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[3xy-{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=3} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[x]_{x=0}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $a=1.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18 & =f(1,b)\\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ 18 & =(3b-2\\cdot 1)\\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ 4 & =3b-2 \\\\ 6 & =3b \\\\ b & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(1,2)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $b=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481402 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166884 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b43eea5b-9da3-5fe0-a28c-cc01aed3627a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Average Value of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b114397-6f41-5b17-af82-49b577e90f08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480940} {:answer/id #uuid "7a750c12-33ce-51e4-9a9c-051ed46ee042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480963} {:answer/id #uuid "b584152a-4a72-5fae-8aff-5821f4148d42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481085} {:answer/id #uuid "bc66edf2-7bc3-52d4-a000-d458e2d36c4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481098} {:answer/id #uuid "c21ae479-8299-5656-a0a9-95c5b26e7361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b584152a-4a72-5fae-8aff-5821f4148d42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481085} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9685638-c235-52d8-a829-db7d8915b067" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a3d12aa9-7854-5ac5-a883-81ef95d3c722" :question/math-academy-id "q-163231" :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)=3{x}^{2}-y$ on the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le 3\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=2\\cdot 2=4.\n$$\n\nApplying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}-y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(3{x}^{2}-y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[3{x}^{2}y-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=1}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[2{x}^{3}-4x]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2\\cdot {2}^{3}-4\\cdot 2\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481348 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166884 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b43eea5b-9da3-5fe0-a28c-cc01aed3627a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Average Value of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "069138e9-203c-5336-a303-692fb1a04b6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480750} {:answer/id #uuid "3be8edd8-c7ec-5754-a981-50e1d2d61749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480854} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d41d76-c8a4-5085-9df7-04c8d4913cd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481076} {:answer/id #uuid "c33b5db7-e254-5fd2-94ab-40b1cb417d41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481115} {:answer/id #uuid "f1625dec-f23d-5eb0-987c-e4d1977364f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2d41d76-c8a4-5085-9df7-04c8d4913cd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481076} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d3f6daa-40ec-59fb-8ee1-def277ce39d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0fd9a9b1-ea5c-5d82-ac2e-6cadd4a02789" :question/math-academy-id "q-163234" :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)=6xy$ on the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2{y}^{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}$ given that the area of the region is $\\frac{2}{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2}{3})}{∬}_{D}6xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2{y}^{2}}6xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[3{x}^{2}y]_{x=0}^{x=2{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}12{y}^{5}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[2{y}^{6}]_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(2-0) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481411 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166884 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b43eea5b-9da3-5fe0-a28c-cc01aed3627a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Average Value of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b7b96a3-3ee8-51d8-bf7e-f10bc137dc77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480796} {:answer/id #uuid "5ea1906a-4d23-5e92-aa42-2516002c4c66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480915} {:answer/id #uuid "654022a5-61f4-581f-8b07-c11340a40a55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480925} {:answer/id #uuid "e98d6b52-9796-58a4-ba09-031961cb2020" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481186} {:answer/id #uuid "fc2a60a5-710d-5d16-938e-a0bd54aeac9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "654022a5-61f4-581f-8b07-c11340a40a55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480925} :answer-field/id #uuid "da8ec83a-e269-5c73-bf54-b07a27aefc49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bbfd9077-09aa-55c5-a7ec-ce238f7922af" :question/math-academy-id "q-163240" :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)=y+{x}^{2}$ on the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,x\\le y\\le 2x\\}$ given that the area of the region is $2.$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{∬}_{D}y+{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x}^{2x}y+{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+{x}^{2}y]_{y=x}^{y=2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{x}^{3}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(8-0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481408 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166885 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4e5deee-e910-5bb1-9d9a-0b24886ab6c1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Average Value of a Function Over a Polar Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d66d8b4-c0c5-583c-9422-a56fec563fa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[8]{e}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480858} {:answer/id #uuid "9d49fd2e-9748-5870-a942-7855eab45dc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\sqrt[4]{e}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481041} {:answer/id #uuid "aaec5f62-9f91-57f5-b958-6eca6bd1c1da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(\\sqrt[4]{e}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481061} {:answer/id #uuid "c378d23d-c433-57b0-a302-3ca7d194c79d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(\\sqrt[2]{e}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481117} {:answer/id #uuid "ff8cb142-efbd-5919-b474-a1189a94a948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\sqrt[2]{e}+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aaec5f62-9f91-57f5-b958-6eca6bd1c1da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(\\sqrt[4]{e}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481061} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1136c19-2541-5007-b027-75b59e3253ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2ff4fa5-a334-50b5-9869-f8e569ec5011" :question/math-academy-id "q-163256" :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ on the disk of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at the origin." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\pi \\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\frac{1}{2},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{[\\frac{{e}^{{r}^{2}}}{2}]}_{0}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\frac{{e}^{1/4}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt[4]{e}-1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt[4]{e}-1}{2}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt[4]{e}-1}{2}(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt[4]{e}-1)\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\cdot (\\sqrt[4]{e}-1)\\pi =4(\\sqrt[4]{e}-1).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481357 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166885 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4e5deee-e910-5bb1-9d9a-0b24886ab6c1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Average Value of a Function Over a Polar Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e360105-eb66-5367-814d-689677702f6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480975} {:answer/id #uuid "9e9f7126-1aec-56cb-a084-4f34d28dafc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481043} {:answer/id #uuid "a79440e4-ebcc-5811-a667-c1e64c7db96b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481055} {:answer/id #uuid "c27b400b-e962-57cc-9be7-11f6f93220fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481111} {:answer/id #uuid "d99527b4-de43-5d96-9eda-144ff935847e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481146}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e360105-eb66-5367-814d-689677702f6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480975} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a10b6cc-8155-5c1e-bf70-aecfcd94d2ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3b9226c2-67e0-5858-9c64-ae46c4147a95" :question/math-academy-id "q-163257" :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)={y}^{2}$ on the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}=4\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (4-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{[\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =2[(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)] \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\cdot 4\\pi =1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281653 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166885 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4e5deee-e910-5bb1-9d9a-0b24886ab6c1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Average Value of a Function Over a Polar Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07614726-b468-5358-bc84-48ba609cfd04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278660} {:answer/id #uuid "14b4450b-952d-5300-8f14-fe1403fe104c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278776} {:answer/id #uuid "5f42bacb-1dcd-5151-b6dd-29e307c8e188" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279465} {:answer/id #uuid "7821b0ff-67a0-50c1-b841-8830b1518869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279664} {:answer/id #uuid "ff5dd293-f356-5370-a825-276c22c64e46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07614726-b468-5358-bc84-48ba609cfd04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278660} :answer-field/id #uuid "e23f56f9-33c4-58f2-aed2-d65d0e9b71dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281227}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9470a948-0cd8-55e7-86d6-65b250b7e800" :question/math-academy-id "q-163260" :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ on the portion of the disk of radius $3$ centered at the origin, where $x\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region $D:$ \n\n![](images/25/25c1f00e588ddaa130f854d9f4e9dfe9b1158d7952a01bae8108ea4b94d3c347.png)\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}=\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{2}{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+1}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{2r}{{r}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)]_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡10-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡10{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡10[\\theta ]_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡10(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{2}{9\\pi }\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡10=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}{9}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481407 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166885 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4e5deee-e910-5bb1-9d9a-0b24886ab6c1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Average Value of a Function Over a Polar Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07e40d47-7a70-5f2c-ba24-6a855b1d6eb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{16}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480757} {:answer/id #uuid "43bff162-e0e6-5e6e-b559-7547ee7c72a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480868} {:answer/id #uuid "e1b2263d-a0be-566e-8984-74cc5d983449" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{16}{5\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481164} {:answer/id #uuid "e83a9fc3-1293-5456-b3e0-ca1e41eb01ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481182} {:answer/id #uuid "e979451f-00ff-5ed9-83e8-7aff07a87414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{32}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481185}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e979451f-00ff-5ed9-83e8-7aff07a87414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{32}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481185} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e8c2182-159c-53aa-a9ec-6a0270a24944" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b0a81f04-6f59-512d-aa20-c54e3b26a51d" :question/math-academy-id "q-163277" :question/problem "![](images/06/06a853735916ae3219eea02fa7e7e30d9e941106600cd5b642d951f553aace59.png)\n\nFind the average value of the function $f(x,y)=5y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ on the semicircle shown above." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at \n$(1,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that is mind, let's now write the $y=-\\sqrt{2x-{x}^{2}}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\sqrt{2x-{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2x-{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2x \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}5y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}5r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }5{r}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }5{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [{r}^{5}]_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ((2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{5}-0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{5}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32{[-\\frac{1}{6}{cos}^{6}⁡\\theta ]}_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{3}[{cos}^{6}⁡(0)-{cos}^{6}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})] \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{3}(1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}\\cdot (-\\frac{16}{3})=-\\frac{32}{3\\pi }.\n$$"}]}
